--- !u!114 &2105633390
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 2105633388}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.TextMeshPro::TMPro.TextMeshProUGUI
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: 'PERSONAL NOTES / RESEARCH



    School Bullying and Social Withdrawal



    Repeated
    bullying does not always leave visible signs. A student may continue attending
    classes, completing assignments, and speaking normally while gradually withdrawing
    from the people around them.



    <color=#8A3F4D><b>One</b></color> incident
    may appear insignificant when viewed alone. A missing notebook, a cruel message,
    laughter from the back of the classroom. But when these things happen repeatedly,
    the victim may begin to expect hostility even in ordinary situations.



    Lucia
    stopped talking about school sometime in October.



    I don''t remember exactly
    when.





    Depression and Changes in Behaviour



    Depression does not
    always look like sadness. Changes may appear gradually: loss of interest, difficulty
    sleeping, irritability, exhaustion, isolation, or an inability to enjoy things
    that once felt comforting.



    <color=#8A3F4D><b>Three</b></color> weeks ago,
    she stopped coming into my room at night.



    She used to do that whenever
    she couldn''t sleep.



    I thought she was finally doing better.





    Notes
    About the Arguments



    Mom and Dad argued again downstairs.



    It started
    over money. At least, I think it did.



    Dad said they had already done everything
    they could for us. Mom told him to lower his voice because Lucia was still awake.



    <color=#8A3F4D><b>No</b></color>
    people came upstairs to check.



    I could hear Lucia moving in the room next
    to mine.



    Then everything went quiet.





    Things I Should Remember



    People
    don''t suddenly become strangers.



    There are usually signs.



    Missed
    meals. Closed doors. Unanswered messages. Lights left on until morning. Things
    that seem meaningless until you start putting them together.



    <color=#8A3F4D><b>Five</b></color>
    minutes.



    That''s how long I stood outside Lucia''s door that night.



    I
    don''t know why I didn''t knock.



    I don''t know why I''m writing this down.



    I
    don''t know why I keep reading it.



    Maybe I''m afraid I''ll forget.



    Maybe
    I already did.'
